   "subject": "Re: Distance sampling flock",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word;line-break:after-white-space\">Hi Cheryl,<div><br></div><div>There is a distance sampling google group, like this one, see here: <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/g/distance-sampling\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/g/distance-sampling&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792487085460000&amp;usg=AOvVaw292wJwXY88FSNy2lnuWBEW\">https://groups.google.com/g/distance-sampling</a></div><div><br></div><div>It does depend on whether flocks are multi species or single species (there was a post on the DS group about this recently). Normally you’d measure to the centre of the flock and have flock-size as a covariate. </div><div><br></div><div>HTH,</div><div><br></div><div>Jo<br><div><br><blockquote type=\"cite\"></blockquote></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word;line-break:after-white-space\"><div><div><blockquote type=\"cite\"><div>On 24 Nov 2020, at 1:00 pm, Cheryl Lohr &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">chery...@dbca.wa.gov.au</a>&gt; wrote:</div><br></blockquote></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word;line-break:after-white-space\"><div><div><blockquote type=\"cite\"><div><div style=\"font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px;font-style:normal;font-variant-caps:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;text-decoration:none\"><div style=\"margin:0cm;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">Hi folks,<u></u><u></u></div><div style=\"margin:0cm;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">I have to run some distance sampling analysis on flocks of birds. While this might not be the most appropriate forum to ask there are some really smart people here. Can anyone point me in the direction of some instructions on how to deal with flocks of birds that effectively turn into duplicate records?<u></u><u></u></div><div style=\"margin:0cm;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">Thanks,<u></u><u></u></div><div style=\"margin:0cm;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">Cheryl<u></u><u></u></div><div style=\"margin:0cm;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><u></u> <u></u></div><div style=\"margin:0cm;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span>Dr Cheryl Lohr<u></u><u></u></span></div><div style=\"margin:0cm;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span>Animal Science Program<u></u><u></u></span></div><div style=\"margin:0cm;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span>Biodiversity and Conservation Science<u></u><u></u></span></div><div style=\"margin:0cm;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span>Western Australia Department of Biodiversity Conservation and Attractions<u></u><u></u></span></div><div style=\"margin:0cm;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span>Phone: 08 94055750<u></u><u></u></span></div><div style=\"margin:0cm;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span>Mobile: 0407335004<u></u><u></u></span></div><div style=\"margin:0cm;font-size:11pt;font